(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["interpretation-of-medical-literature-exam-1-introduction-bias-validity-quiz"]={"title":"Introduction, Bias and Validity Quiz 1 — Interpretation of Medical Literature","category":"Interpretation of Medical Literature","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which two questions should a provider ask when presented with a study?","choices":["Was the study published in a high-impact journal, and how often is it cited?","How large was the sample, and how long did the study run?","Are the conclusions valid for the sample, and does the sample represent my patients?","Who funded the study, and were the results statistically significant?"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and the two map onto the two kinds of validity: the first is internal validity, the second external validity, which is why both have to be asked. — 1 PAJ5512 Introduction Bias Validity MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"What characterizes nervousness-based medicine?","choices":["Fear of litigation driving over-investigation","Being too timid to make any medical decision","Bravado standing in for evidence","Leaving the decision in the hands of the Almighty"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and it is distinct from diffidence-based medicine, where the practitioner is too timid to make any decision at all rather than moved to do too much. Overtreatment follows from the same fear. — 1 PAJ5512 Introduction Bias Validity MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"What is bias in medical research?","choices":["Systematic error producing conclusions that differ from the true results","Random variation producing conclusions that differ from the true results","A deliberate attempt by investigators to mislead readers","A difference between two groups that reaches statistical significance"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and the word systematic separates it from chance: bias pushes results consistently in one direction, whereas random variation is equally likely to go either way. — 1 PAJ5512 Introduction Bias Validity MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"Which purposes does epidemiology serve in public health practice?","choices":["Establishing the diagnosis in an individual patient presenting to clinic","Discovering agent, host and environmental factors that affect health","Determining the appropriate dose of a medication for a given patient","Setting the reimbursement rates paid to hospitals and clinics"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and two more complete the set: identifying the population segments at greatest risk, and evaluating whether health programs and services actually improve population health. Ranking the relative importance of causes of illness, disability and death belongs to it too. — 1 PAJ5512 Introduction Bias Validity MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"What is procedural bias?","choices":["Participants drop out before the study is complete","Interview or questionnaire results are affected by the method of delivery","Respondents differ systematically from non-respondents","Care differs between the groups in ways other than the intervention"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and the example is pointed: administering a questionnaire after an operation while the patient is still under the after-effects of anesthesia. — 1 PAJ5512 Introduction Bias Validity MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"How do a population and a sample differ?","choices":["A population is the people studied; a sample is everyone else","A population is always larger than one million people","A population is everyone in a defined setting; a sample is a subset of it","A sample is chosen only when the population cannot be identified"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and the definition of a population turns on the defining characteristic or setting, which is what determines whether a given sample represents it. — 1 PAJ5512 Introduction Bias Validity MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"What is external validity also called?","choices":["Generalisability","Reliability","Internal validity","Significance"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and it is rarely determined from a single study, which is why one trial supporting an intervention does not establish that it will work in a different setting. — 1 PAJ5512 Introduction Bias Validity MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"What is another name for an extraneous variable?","choices":["A covariate","A dependent variable","An independent variable","A sample"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and the definition is functional: it is a variable that may affect the relationship between the independent and dependent variables, which is what makes it worth identifying. — 1 PAJ5512 Introduction Bias Validity MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"What protects against performance bias?","choices":["Increasing the sample size","Disclosing conflicts of interest","Publishing the study protocol in advance","Blinding of study participants"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and the mechanism follows directly from the example: a participant who does not know they are on placebo has no reason to go looking for other treatment. — 1 PAJ5512 Introduction Bias Validity MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"What is performance bias?","choices":["The measurement method produces systematically incorrect values","Care differs between the groups in ways other than the topic of interest","Groups differ at baseline in ways that affect the outcome","Only favorable results are made available"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and the example is that participants in a control group may seek other treatments once they know they are receiving a placebo, which changes their care independently of the trial. — 1 PAJ5512 Introduction Bias Validity MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"Which clinical question does the term Abnormality address?","choices":["How often does the disease occur?","What factors are associated with increased risk?","What are the consequences of having the disease?","Is the patient sick or well?"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and it sits alongside Frequency, which asks how often a disease occurs, as one of the questions that has to be settled before any answer about this patient is possible. — 1 PAJ5512 Introduction Bias Validity MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"What is noted about determining external validity?","choices":["It follows automatically once internal validity is established","It is established by the size of the sample alone","It is rarely determined from a single study","It is determined by the journal in which the study appears"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and the practical consequence is that a result has to be reproduced in other settings and populations before it can be relied on outside the original one. — 1 PAJ5512 Introduction Bias Validity MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"How is epidemiology defined?","choices":["The study of disease mechanisms at the cellular and molecular level","The study of how drugs are absorbed, distributed and eliminated","The distribution and determinants of health-related states in populations","The statistical analysis of data collected during a single clinical trial"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and the definition does not stop at studying: it extends to applying that study to the control of health problems, which is what makes it a practical discipline rather than a descriptive one. — 1 PAJ5512 Introduction Bias Validity MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"What is reporting bias?","choices":["Research without significant results is less likely to be published","The method of questionnaire delivery distorts the answers","Financial considerations affect the objectivity of the investigators","Only a subset of all relevant data is made available"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and it is distinct from publication bias: here the study appears, but not all of what it found appears with it. — 1 PAJ5512 Introduction Bias Validity MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"Which two questions fall under Prevention?","choices":["What conditions lead to the disease, and what are its origins?","How often does the disease occur, and who is most at risk?","Does prevention stop disease arising, and does early detection help?","Is the patient sick or well, and what happens to them next?"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and the pairing matters because the two are different claims: stopping disease from arising is not the same as finding it sooner once it has. — 1 PAJ5512 Introduction Bias Validity MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Which agency, together with state and county health departments, operates the United States disease reporting system?","choices":["The United Nations","The National Center for Health Statistics","The World Health Organization","The Centers for Disease Control and Prevention"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Reportable diseases flow from clinicians through state and county health departments to that agency, which is what makes notifiable-disease lists a usable national data source. — 1 PAJ5512 Introduction Bias Validity MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"What can be done about chance in a study?","choices":["It can be eliminated entirely by a sufficiently large sample","It can be corrected for during data analysis like any other error","It can be estimated statistically and reduced, but never eliminated","It is present only in studies that also contain bias"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and it may coexist with bias, so demonstrating that a result is unlikely to be due to chance does not establish that it is free of systematic error. Good study design is what reduces it. — 1 PAJ5512 Introduction Bias Validity MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"What is internal validity?","choices":["The degree to which results are also true in other clinical settings","The degree to which results are correct for the sample of patients studied","The degree to which a measurement instrument gives consistent readings","The proportion of enrolled participants who completed the study"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and it is the prior question: results that are not correct for the people actually studied cannot be true of anyone else either. — 1 PAJ5512 Introduction Bias Validity MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"Why does non-response bias matter?","choices":["The sample size falls below the level needed for analysis","The questionnaire was delivered at an inappropriate moment","Responders may differ from non-respondents","Results that are not statistically significant go unpublished"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and the consequence is about generalization: a survey answered by an unrepresentative subset describes that subset rather than the whole population. — 1 PAJ5512 Introduction Bias Validity MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"From what three sources are answers to clinical questions drawn?","choices":["Experience, intuition and consensus","Guidelines, textbooks and colleagues","Knowledge, cost and patient preference","Experience, knowledge and clinical research"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and the three are complementary: leaning on experience alone is the eminence-based reasoning that evidence-based practice was formulated against. — 1 PAJ5512 Introduction Bias Validity MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"What does the United States Vital Statistics System collect?","choices":["Reportable communicable diseases only","Births, deaths, causes of death, marriages and divorces","Hospital emergency department and discharge data","Self-reported health behaviors gathered by telephone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. It is the registration system for life events, which is distinct from the population census and from the periodic health surveys run by the National Center for Health Statistics. — 1 PAJ5512 Introduction Bias Validity MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"What drives publication bias?","choices":["Research without significant results is less likely to be published","Investigators disclose only a subset of the data they collected","Participants drop out before the study is complete","Journals publish only research funded by government agencies"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and two forces sustain it: journal editor preference, and sponsors being less likely to support research unfavorable to them or to their product. — 1 PAJ5512 Introduction Bias Validity MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"What kind of data does the National Center for Health Statistics gather?","choices":["Continuous registration of every birth and death","Periodic surveys of disease and disability","Disease-specific data collected by state governments","Financial data on health care spending by household"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Its examples include the National Health Interview Survey and the National Health and Nutrition Examination Survey, both periodic rather than continuous registrations. — 1 PAJ5512 Introduction Bias Validity MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"What is chance in medical research?","choices":["Systematic error producing conclusions that differ from the true results","A deliberate decision to report only favorable findings","The probability that a patient will develop a given disease","Random variation producing conclusions that differ from the true results"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and the crucial property is that it is random: it is equally likely to produce a result higher or lower than the truth, unlike bias, which pushes one way. — 1 PAJ5512 Introduction Bias Validity MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"In a medication trial, the placebo control group turns out to be older on average than the experimental group. What does this represent?","choices":["Age acting as a confounding variable","Measurement bias in recording age","Attrition effect","Reporting bias by the investigators"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, because age is plausibly associated with both group membership and the outcome, so its effect cannot be separated from the effect of the medication being tested. — 1 PAJ5512 Introduction Bias Validity MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"At which levels are practice guidelines produced?","choices":["Hospital or clinic, national, and professional bodies","Only by national government agencies","Only by the individual treating clinician","Only by the journals that publish original research"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, with the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention among the national sources and bodies such as the American Academy of Pediatrics among the professional organizations. — 1 PAJ5512 Introduction Bias Validity MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"What is the difficulty with applying statistical analysis to data from poorly designed research?","choices":["Statistical analysis cannot be performed on such data at all","It may give a false sense of respectability to the research","It removes the effect of bias from the results","It converts a descriptive study into an explanatory one"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and the reason it is a trap is that no analysis can correct for unknown biases, so the statistics look rigorous while resting on data that is not. — 1 PAJ5512 Introduction Bias Validity MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"Besides registries and surveys, which other data sources are named?","choices":["Peer-reviewed journal articles","Practice guidelines issued by professional organizations","Textbooks of clinical epidemiology","Hospital data such as admissions and discharges, and clinic data"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and their value is that they already exist as a by-product of care, though they describe only the people who sought that care. Emergency department records belong to the same category. — 1 PAJ5512 Introduction Bias Validity MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"What follows from the fact that a study has the potential for bias?","choices":["Bias should be considered, but potential does not always mean it is present","The study's conclusions must be rejected","The study's conclusions can be accepted without further scrutiny","Statistical analysis will correct for it"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and the practical consequence is that bias is often detectable when reading an article, so the reader's job is to look for it rather than to assume it either way. — 1 PAJ5512 Introduction Bias Validity MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"What defines confounding?","choices":["The covariate must be the direct cause of the disease","The covariate's effect cannot be separated from the variable studied","The measurement instrument produces consistently high readings","Participants in the control group seek other treatments"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and the covariate must be associated with both the exposure and the outcome, which is what allows it to masquerade as the relationship under investigation. — 1 PAJ5512 Introduction Bias Validity MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 42"}]};
